        {
          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01QDgRpVHs99EzRPBEXXk2za",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022concepts/idempotency-keys.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Idempotency Keys\\ndescription: A client-generated unique identifier attached to a request to enable safe retries of non-idempotent operations without duplication.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Idempotency\\n  - concept/Resilience\\n  - pattern/Retry-Safety\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Single source covering the basic mechanism and expiry-policy operational concern.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\nIdempotency keys allow a client to safely retry a non-idempotent operation (such as charging a card) by attaching a client-generated unique key to the request. The server maintains a deduplication table: when a request with the same key arrives, the server recognizes the replay and returns the original result instead of performing the action a second time.\\n\\n## Operational Concern: Expiry Policy\\n\\nA critical operational requirement for idempotency keys is a **server-side expiry policy** for the deduplication table. Without one, the table grows unbounded: every unique key ever generated is stored forever, consuming memory and increasing lookup latency over time.\\n\\nThe expiry policy must balance two competing concerns:\\n\\n- **Safety**: Keys must remain in the dedup table long enough to catch all legitimate retries (typically aligned with the client\\u0027s retry window and network timeout expectations).\\n- **Resource efficiency**: Keys older than the maximum plausible retry window should be discarded to prevent table bloat.\\n\\nA reasonable heuristic is to expire keys after a duration that exceeds the longest expected retry period for your use case \\u2014 for example, 24 hours for a payment system, or minutes for a short-lived API interaction.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: eval://steering-adoption/sample-10\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
